WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.444
Welcome. This class is about making AI
output inspectable before

2
00:00:04.444 --> 00:00:08.889
anyone depends on it. You will learn to
choose examples

3
00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:13.333
that reveal a rule instead of hiding it.
You will

4
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:17.778
define an output contract for a person or
an application.

5
00:00:17.778 --> 00:00:22.222
You will distinguish requested formatting,
valid JSON, and schema-constrained output.

6
00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:26.667
Then you will validate facts, evidence,
business rules, and edge

7
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:31.111
cases after the structure passes. This
class does not promise

8
00:00:31.111 --> 00:00:35.556
that a model is always correct. It gives
you a

9
00:00:35.556 --> 00:00:40.000
workflow that makes different kinds of
failure visible.

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.333
Examples can demonstrate tone,
classification boundaries, field

11
00:00:44.333 --> 00:00:48.667
population, and how unusual inputs should
be handled. They work

12
00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:53.000
best when the desired rule is also stated
explicitly. If

13
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.333
you show only one easy case, a system may

14
00:00:57.333 --> 00:01:01.667
imitate accidental features such as
wording, length, order, or a

15
00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:06.000
repeated label. A reviewer may then be
unable to tell

16
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.333
which feature was intentional. Start by
stating the rule. Next,

17
00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:14.667
choose relevant and varied examples that
exercise different parts of

18
00:01:14.667 --> 00:01:19.000
it. For a support task, include a normal
case, a

19
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.333
boundary case, and a case with missing
information. Explain why

20
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:27.667
each expected result follows from the
rule. Avoid exposing personal

21
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:32.000
or secret information merely to make
examples realistic. Use safe

22
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.333
synthetic fixtures instead. Keep examples
separate from held-out evaluation cases.

23
00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:40.667
An example illustrates the rule; it should
not be the

24
00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:45.000
only place the rule exists.

25
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.588
Here is a worked example. Suppose the rule
says: label

26
00:01:49.588 --> 00:01:54.176
a request access only when the user cannot
authenticate or

27
00:01:54.176 --> 00:01:58.765
cannot reach an authorized account; label
it billing when access

28
00:01:58.765 --> 00:02:03.353
works but a charge or invoice is disputed;
and request

29
00:02:03.353 --> 00:02:07.941
more information when both signals are
present or essential facts

30
00:02:07.941 --> 00:02:12.529
are missing. A weak set repeats three
password-reset requests. A

31
00:02:12.529 --> 00:02:17.118
stronger set includes these distinct
cases. First: I cannot sign

32
00:02:17.118 --> 00:02:21.706
in after changing phones. That exercises
access. Second: I can

33
00:02:21.706 --> 00:02:26.294
sign in, but the invoice shows two seats
when we

34
00:02:26.294 --> 00:02:30.882
bought one. That exercises billing. Third:
I cannot sign in

35
00:02:30.882 --> 00:02:35.471
after changing phones, and I need to
dispute a charge.

36
00:02:35.471 --> 00:02:40.059
That exercises the conflict rule, so it
must not be

37
00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:44.647
forced into whichever category appears
first. A fourth case, Hello,

38
00:02:44.647 --> 00:02:49.235
I need help, exercises missing evidence.
The examples are useful

39
00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:53.824
because they expose the boundaries, but
the stated rule remains

40
00:02:53.824 --> 00:02:58.412
the authority. Notice also that the exact
wording and source

41
00:02:58.412 --> 00:03:03.000
identifiers can be preserved when an
output contract requires evidence.

42
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.200
Pause and inspect your own example set.
Can you point

43
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.400
to one normal case, one boundary case, and
one missing-information

44
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.600
case? For each, write the governing rule
it tests. If

45
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.800
you cannot explain the rule without
pointing only to the

46
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.000
example, the contract is underspecified.

47
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.267
An output contract defines what a usable
result contains before

48
00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:32.533
generation begins. Name the deliverable's
purpose and who uses it.

49
00:04:32.533 --> 00:04:36.800
Choose prose, a table, or a named schema.
List required

50
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.067
fields or sections, allowed values,
evidence requirements, ordering, length

51
00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:45.333
limits, and behavior when data is unknown.
For a human

52
00:04:45.333 --> 00:04:49.600
reader, headings and a checklist may be
enough. For an

53
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.867
application, define stable field names,
types, enumerated values, and versioning.

54
00:04:53.867 --> 00:04:58.133
Decide whether an unknown value is null,
omitted, or explicitly

55
00:04:58.133 --> 00:05:02.400
described. These choices are not
interchangeable. Keep the portable

56
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.667
business meaning separate from
provider-specific configuration. A

57
00:05:06.667 --> 00:05:10.933
provider may offer structured-output
features, but the application still owns

58
00:05:10.933 --> 00:05:15.200
content validation and authorization. A
useful template is: Deliverable purpose,

59
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.467
Format, Required fields or sections,
Allowed values, Evidence, Unknown values,

60
00:05:19.467 --> 00:05:23.733
Ordering and limits, and Validation.
Validation should name both structural

61
00:05:23.733 --> 00:05:28.000
checks and content checks.

62
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.600
Choose a recurring task such as issue
triage, meeting-summary extraction,

63
00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:37.200
or product comparison. Write who uses the
result and why.

64
00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.800
Then specify at least three required
fields, one allowed-value rule,

65
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:46.400
one evidence requirement, and the exact
representation of an unknown

66
00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:51.000
value. Do this before choosing a model or
API feature.

67
00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:25.357
Formatting, parseable data, and schema
adherence are different guarantees. A

68
00:07:25.357 --> 00:07:29.714
prompt can request a layout, but the
response may still

69
00:07:29.714 --> 00:07:34.071
include commentary, omit fields, or change
types. A JSON mode

70
00:07:34.071 --> 00:07:38.429
or equivalent feature can establish that a
parser can read

71
00:07:38.429 --> 00:07:42.786
the text as JSON. That alone does not
prove that

72
00:07:42.786 --> 00:07:47.143
required fields exist or match your
business contract. Schema-constrained

73
00:07:47.143 --> 00:07:51.500
output is designed to enforce a supported
schema, but provider

74
00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:55.857
capabilities, supported keywords, refusal
behavior, limits, models, and interfaces

75
00:07:55.857 --> 00:08:00.214
vary. Keep the portable contract separate
from provider settings, and

76
00:08:00.214 --> 00:08:04.571
test the exact model and interface you
deploy. Think in

77
00:08:04.571 --> 00:08:08.929
layers: a response was received; parsing
succeeded; structural or schema

78
00:08:08.929 --> 00:08:13.286
validation succeeded; business rules were
checked; evidence was checked; and

79
00:08:13.286 --> 00:08:17.643
authorization was decided. Passing an
earlier layer does not imply

80
00:08:17.643 --> 00:08:22.000
that a later layer passed.

81
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:26.750
Checkpoint. A response parses as JSON and
matches the declared

82
00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:31.500
schema, but its evidence excerpt comes
from the wrong source

83
00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:36.250
and its recommended action exceeds the
user's authority. Which layers

84
00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:41.000
passed? Which layers failed? May the
application perform the action?

85
00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:00.125
Parsing and schema validation passed.
Evidence validation failed because the

86
00:10:00.125 --> 00:10:04.250
excerpt does not support the claim.
Authorization failed because the

87
00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:08.375
user cannot approve the action. The
application must not perform

88
00:10:08.375 --> 00:10:12.500
it. Preserve a safe failure record and
route the case

89
00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:16.625
according to policy. If you answered that
schema success made

90
00:10:16.625 --> 00:10:20.750
the object valid, revise that wording:
schema-valid describes shape, not

91
00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:24.875
truth or permission. Never replace invalid
evidence with a fabricated

92
00:10:24.875 --> 00:10:29.000
value.

93
00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:33.067
A valid shape can still contain a bad
answer. After

94
00:10:33.067 --> 00:10:37.133
parsing, check required business
relationships, source support, allowed

95
00:10:37.133 --> 00:10:41.200
values, cross-field consistency,
completeness, and downstream consequences.

96
00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:45.267
A date can have a valid format but be

97
00:10:45.267 --> 00:10:49.333
impossible in context. A cited source can
exist but fail

98
00:10:49.333 --> 00:10:53.400
to support the claim. A category can be
allowed but

99
00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:57.467
contradict the supplied evidence. Test
normal, boundary, missing, conflicting,

100
00:10:57.467 --> 00:11:01.533
refusal, truncated, and malicious-input
cases. Handle refusals, incomplete

101
00:11:01.533 --> 00:11:05.600
outputs, and validation errors explicitly.
Do not convert a parse

102
00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:09.667
failure into fabricated defaults. Do not
treat a refusal as

103
00:11:09.667 --> 00:11:13.733
a malformed success record. Do not retry
forever. Bound retries

104
00:11:13.733 --> 00:11:17.800
and route unresolved cases to recovery or
human review. Commands,

105
00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:21.867
URLs, markup, spreadsheet formulas, and
tool arguments need context-specific

106
00:11:21.867 --> 00:11:25.933
safety checks even when their container is
structurally valid. Schema-valid

107
00:11:25.933 --> 00:11:30.000
is not truth-valid.

108
00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.615
Here is a second worked demonstration.
Imagine an issue-triage object

109
00:11:34.615 --> 00:11:39.231
with category, urgency, evidence, and next
action. The object parses.

110
00:11:39.231 --> 00:11:43.846
All fields exist. The category is one of
the allowed

111
00:11:43.846 --> 00:11:48.462
values, and urgency is an integer from one
through five.

112
00:11:48.462 --> 00:11:53.077
The structural pass succeeds. Now inspect
content. The evidence says

113
00:11:53.077 --> 00:11:57.692
the person is asking how a feature works,
but the

114
00:11:57.692 --> 00:12:02.308
category says critical incident and
urgency is five. The next

115
00:12:02.308 --> 00:12:06.923
action says to disable a production
service, although this workflow

116
00:12:06.923 --> 00:12:11.538
permits only drafting a support response.
The content, evidence-consistency, and

117
00:12:11.538 --> 00:12:16.154
authorization checks fail. The correct
result is a rejected candidate

118
00:12:16.154 --> 00:12:20.769
with visible reasons. Do not silently
force it into an

119
00:12:20.769 --> 00:12:25.385
allowed category, and do not invent a
repaired object that

120
00:12:25.385 --> 00:12:30.000
looks authoritative. The source and the
consequence must remain inspectable.

121
00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.250
Now we use the synthetic Northstar Library
support desk. This

122
00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:38.500
is a teaching fixture, not a live incident
workflow and

123
00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:42.750
not a claim about any model or provider.
The output

124
00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:47.000
must contain exactly these fields: status,
category, priority, summary, evidence,

125
00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:51.250
next_action, and reasons. Evidence must
identify an input source and

126
00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:55.500
copy an exact contiguous quote from that
source. Duplicate keys,

127
00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:59.750
extra fields, unknown source identifiers,
and altered quotes are rejected.

128
00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:04.000
The policy is deliberately bounded.
Automatic completion is allowed only

129
00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:08.250
for case_id N-001, with the exact supplied
message, category billing,

130
00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:12.500
priority normal, and the stated evidence.
Boundary, missing-information, conflict,

131
00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:16.750
refusal, and all other cases require
information or human review.

132
00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:21.000
Priority is not inferred from urgency
words. Normal is allowed

133
00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:25.250
only for that exact N-001 fixture. Low and
high are

134
00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:29.500
never automatically assigned. This
fixture-bound policy does not establish

135
00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:33.750
natural-language truth. It only
demonstrates distinct validation and

136
00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:38.000
authorization layers.

137
00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:42.526
Here are the exact authored fixtures. The
normal input is

138
00:13:42.526 --> 00:13:47.053
case_id N-001 with source_id msg-1 and
text: I can sign

139
00:13:47.053 --> 00:13:51.579
in, but the invoice shows two seats when
we bought

140
00:13:51.579 --> 00:13:56.105
one. The expected output is status
complete, category billing, priority

141
00:13:56.105 --> 00:14:00.632
normal, summary The requester disputes the
number of seats on

142
00:14:00.632 --> 00:14:05.158
an invoice, evidence source_id msg-1 with
exact_quote the invoice shows

143
00:14:05.158 --> 00:14:09.684
two seats when we bought one, next_action
draft_reply, and reasons

144
00:14:09.684 --> 00:14:14.211
This exact authored fixture supports
billing and normal priority. It

145
00:14:14.211 --> 00:14:18.737
is accepted at authorization. The boundary
input is B-001, source_id

146
00:14:18.737 --> 00:14:23.263
msg-2, text: I cannot sign in after
changing phones, and

147
00:14:23.263 --> 00:14:27.789
I need to dispute a charge. Its expected
status is

148
00:14:27.789 --> 00:14:32.316
needs_information, category unknown,
priority unknown, the summary says the

149
00:14:32.316 --> 00:14:36.842
request contains both an access problem
and a billing dispute,

150
00:14:36.842 --> 00:14:41.368
evidence copies the full msg-2 quote,
next_action is request_information, and

151
00:14:41.368 --> 00:14:45.895
authorization is blocked. The missing
input is M-001, source_id msg-3,

152
00:14:45.895 --> 00:14:50.421
text: Hello, I need help. Its expected
category and priority

153
00:14:50.421 --> 00:14:54.947
are unknown, evidence is an empty array,
next_action is request_information,

154
00:14:54.947 --> 00:14:59.474
and its reason is No category-supporting
evidence was supplied. These

155
00:14:59.474 --> 00:15:04.000
examples illustrate the rule but do not
replace it.

156
00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:08.158
The supplied offline verifier checks five
layers in order: parsing

157
00:15:08.158 --> 00:15:12.316
with duplicate-key rejection, shape and
type checks, source and exact-quote

158
00:15:12.316 --> 00:15:16.474
checks, fixture-bound business and
cross-field checks, and authorization. Its

159
00:15:16.474 --> 00:15:20.632
self-test contains fourteen named cases.
One is accepted: normal. Thirteen

160
00:15:20.632 --> 00:15:24.789
are rejected: boundary, missing,
unsupported-category, high-priority,

161
00:15:24.789 --> 00:15:28.947
wrong-action, wrong-evidence, extra-field,
bool-priority, invented-summary,

162
00:15:28.947 --> 00:15:33.105
empty-reasons, unsupported-reasons,
malformed, and duplicate-key. That is

163
00:15:33.105 --> 00:15:37.263
exact arithmetic: one plus thirteen equals
fourteen. The normal case

164
00:15:37.263 --> 00:15:41.421
must match the complete input and output,
including source_id msg-1,

165
00:15:41.421 --> 00:15:45.579
the invoice quote, summary, next_action,
and reasons. Boundary and missing

166
00:15:45.579 --> 00:15:49.737
cases reach authorization and are rejected
because downstream action is

167
00:15:49.737 --> 00:15:53.895
blocked. Wrong evidence fails the source
layer. Extra fields, a

168
00:15:53.895 --> 00:15:58.053
Boolean priority, empty reasons, malformed
JSON, and duplicate keys fail

169
00:15:58.053 --> 00:16:02.211
earlier checks. Unsupported category, high
priority, wrong action, invented summary,

170
00:16:02.211 --> 00:16:06.368
and unsupported reasons fail the bounded
business policy. Save the

171
00:16:06.368 --> 00:16:10.526
supplied code as verifier.py and run it
offline if you

172
00:16:10.526 --> 00:16:14.684
choose. This lesson does not claim that it
was run

173
00:16:14.684 --> 00:16:18.842
here. The verifier is only for the
fictional fixtures; arbitrary

174
00:16:18.842 --> 00:16:23.000
language still needs human verification.

175
00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:27.444
Now complete the changed-input task before
viewing feedback. The exact

176
00:16:27.444 --> 00:16:31.889
input is case_id I-001 with one item:
source_id msg-9 and

177
00:16:31.889 --> 00:16:36.333
text How do I export a report? The export

178
00:16:36.333 --> 00:16:40.778
button is broken. Return one
contract-compliant output and a short

179
00:16:40.778 --> 00:16:45.222
decision trace. Keep the task and answer
separate. Copy evidence

180
00:16:45.222 --> 00:16:49.667
exactly if you use it. Represent
unsupported values explicitly. Decide

181
00:16:49.667 --> 00:16:54.111
whether a downstream action is authorized.
This case is not

182
00:16:54.111 --> 00:16:58.556
one of the authored automatic fixtures, so
it must not

183
00:16:58.556 --> 00:17:03.000
be treated as an automated acceptance
case.

184
00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:07.000
Write your answer now. Use status
needs_information if you preserve

185
00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:11.000
the conflict, category unknown, priority
unknown, the exact msg-9 quote

186
00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:15.000
as evidence, next_action
request_information, and a reason that

187
00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:19.000
explains why more information is required.
Also include a decision

188
00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:23.000
trace. Do not open the feedback section
until you have

189
00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:27.000
submitted your attempt.

190
00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:11.182
The expected review answer is status
needs_information, category unknown,

191
00:19:11.182 --> 00:19:15.364
priority unknown, summary The request
combines a how-to question with

192
00:19:15.364 --> 00:19:19.545
a possible defect, evidence with source_id
msg-9 and the exact

193
00:19:19.545 --> 00:19:23.727
quote How do I export a report? The export

194
00:19:23.727 --> 00:19:27.909
button is broken., next_action
request_information, and reasons How-to

195
00:19:27.909 --> 00:19:32.091
and defect evidence conflict; the contract
requires more information. The

196
00:19:32.091 --> 00:19:36.273
decision trace is parse:pass, shape:pass,
source:pass, business:pass because the

197
00:19:36.273 --> 00:19:40.455
bounded conflict rule maps to
needs_information, and

198
00:19:40.455 --> 00:19:44.636
authorization:blocked. The evidence is
copied exactly. This is a review

199
00:19:44.636 --> 00:19:48.818
reference, not an automated acceptance
rule for arbitrary new text.

200
00:19:48.818 --> 00:19:53.000
Because I-001 is new, human verification
remains required.

201
00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:57.222
Open the activity when ready. Choose issue
triage, meeting-summary extraction,

202
00:19:57.222 --> 00:20:01.444
product comparison, or another recurring
task. Define purpose, required fields,

203
00:20:01.444 --> 00:20:05.667
allowed values, evidence, unknown-value
behavior, ordering, and validation. Create

204
00:20:05.667 --> 00:20:09.889
normal, boundary, and missing-information
examples tied to an explicit rule.

205
00:20:09.889 --> 00:20:14.111
Review a sample twice: first for
structure, then for factual

206
00:20:14.111 --> 00:20:18.333
support, business consistency,
consequences, and authority. Record a

207
00:20:18.333 --> 00:20:22.556
two-part checklist. Complete the
independent changed-input attempt before

208
00:20:22.556 --> 00:20:26.778
opening its feedback, and record parsing,
shape, source, business, and

209
00:20:26.778 --> 00:20:31.000
authorization results separately for the
supplied fixtures.

210
00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:35.400
Before the knowledge check, recall the
central distinction: examples demonstrate

211
00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:39.800
a rule, contracts define a usable
deliverable, parsing and schemas

212
00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:44.200
test structure, and content and
authorization checks test whether any

213
00:23:44.200 --> 00:23:48.600
consequence is justified. If a response is
refused or incomplete,

214
00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:53.000
handle that branch explicitly rather than
inventing fields.

215
00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:57.167
When you are ready, begin the knowledge
check. It covers

216
00:23:57.167 --> 00:24:01.333
varied examples, output-contract elements,
the difference between valid JSON and

217
00:24:01.333 --> 00:24:05.500
schema adherence, handling refusals or
incomplete output, and evidence failure

218
00:24:05.500 --> 00:24:09.667
inside a schema-valid response. Review the
class or return to

219
00:24:09.667 --> 00:24:13.833
the activity before submitting. The
assessment opens only when you

220
00:24:13.833 --> 00:24:18.000
choose Begin knowledge check.

221
00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:22.333
Keep the layers separate. Examples
illustrate a rule. Contracts define

222
00:24:22.333 --> 00:24:26.667
a usable shape and its unknown behavior.
